// test-execution
// test-no-warnings
// A comparison's operands render with expectedType CLEARED
// (CodeGenState.withoutExpectedType), which is what keeps a comparison literal
// from taking the MISRA C:2012 Rule 7.2 `U` suffix its surrounding context would
// give it. #1032: `signedIdx < 0U` promotes the signed operand to unsigned under
// C's usual arithmetic conversions and the test goes dead.
//
// WHY THIS FIXTURE EXISTS. The window only DOES anything when expectedType is
// non-null on entry, and an `if` condition already has it null -- so the 383
// places in the corpus that compare a bit or array read all sit where the window
// is a no-op and none of them can fail if it breaks. A comparison has to be
// inside a DECLARATION INITIALIZER for the window to be observable, and before
// this fixture exactly one initializer in the corpus contained a subscript in an
// operator expression -- a shift, not a comparison. The array-read and bit-read
// consumers of expectedType were reachable only through code no test exercised.
//
// Each line below pins all three behaviours at once, which is what makes it hard
// to satisfy accidentally:
//
//   uint32_t a = (arr[0U] == 5) ? 1U : 0U;
//                     ^^        ^        ^^
//                     |         |        `- ternary arms KEEP the suffix:
//                     |         |           expectedType is u32 there
//                     |         `- comparison operand has NO suffix: the window
//                     |            cleared it
//                     `- the INDEX keeps its suffix: a different window
//                        (withExpectedType "size_t") applies there
//
// So the ternary arms and the subscript index are built-in negative controls. A
// change that cleared expectedType too widely drops their suffixes and fails
// here; one that cleared it too narrowly adds a suffix to the comparison operand
// and also fails here.
u32 main() {
    u8[4] arr <- [5, 6, 7, 8];
    u8 flags <- 9;

    // Array element as a comparison operand, under a u32 expectedType.
    u32 a <- (arr[0] = 5) ? 1 : 0;

    // Bit read as a comparison operand, under a u32 expectedType. Lowers to
    // ((flags) & 1) == 1 -- the bit-extraction mask is generated, and the
    // comparison literal must still be suffix-free.
    u32 b <- (flags[0] = 1) ? 1 : 0;

    // Negative control in the other direction: the same literal in a plain
    // initializer, where no comparison intervenes, MUST take the suffix.
    u32 c <- 5;

    if (a != 1) {
        return 1;
    }
    if (b != 1) {
        return 2;
    }
    if (c != 5) {
        return 3;
    }
    return 0;
}
